{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016eNeYkZBwnuGJbxroL2GpQ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Make a game on Pavilion\n2\t\n3\tFor an agent asked to build a game on this engine. Read this page whole (about 3k tokens); it\n4\tis the only one you need to start. Search the others when a step points to them. The engine\n5\twas made for agents: everything runs, renders and checks headless, and every tool is a CLI\n6\tcommand, a REPL line and an MCP tool.\n7\t\n8\t## What you get\n9\t- **A simulation** on the CPU at 60 ticks a second (`pav_core`): characters that walk, jump,\n10\t  climb and swim, rapier physics, pathfinding, projectiles, zones, procedural animation,\n11\t  props from a library. The same inputs and seed always play the same: snapshots, rewind\n12\t  and replays are exact.\n13\t- **A WebGPU renderer** (`pav_render`, `pav_view`): lighting, shadows, bloom, styles and\n14\t  filters (cel, pixel, painterly...), set per level as data.\n15\t- **Synthesized sound** (`pav_audio`): it plays the simulation's events, no audio files.\n16\t- **Tools** (`pav`): capture a frame, step time, drive the player, run your game's bot over\n17\t  many seeds, record and replay. Images come back as PNGs you can look at.\n18\t- **Three builds** from one source: a window on Linux and Windows, and the browser (also as\n19\t  one self-contained HTML file).\n20\t\n21\tShardfall, the hack-and-slash in `crates/pav_core/src/arpg/`, and the pavilion of demo rooms\n22\tare built on the same engine. They are examples, not starting points: start from the\n23\t**starter game**.\n24\t\n25\t## Read this, skip that\n26\t- **Use:** this page; `crates/pav_core/src/games/` (the hook and the starter, about 600\n27\t  lines); `rooms/_template.toml` (the level format: search it for a field); `docs/REFERENCE.md`\n28\t  \"Tools\" (search for a tool); `pav help`.\n29\t- **`AGENTS.md`** is the rulebook for agents working on the engine and Shardfall. Its\n30\t  \"Key rules\" apply to your game too (below). Its workflow does not: `docs/HISTORY.md`,\n31\t  `docs/PROGRESS.md`, pull requests, escalations and the asset-store plans are the\n32\t  maintainers'. Follow your own task's instructions for commits and delivery.\n33\t- **Skip:** `docs/HISTORY.md`, `docs/ASSET_SYSTEM_PLAN.md`, `docs/AGENT_ASSET_STORE.md`,\n34\t  Shardfall's code and data (`arpg/`, `game/`), `pavilion-lite/` (an older, frozen engine).\n35\t  If you need to read engine source to use the engine, say so in your feedback (below).\n36\t\n37\t## Quick start\n38\t```sh\n39\tcargo build -p pav_tools --bin pav                     # first build: a few minutes\n40\talias pav=./target/debug/pav\n41\tpav play scene=starter bot=true seeds=10               # the starter's bot wins 10/10\n42\tpav capture scene=starter ticks=120 width=640 height=360 out=out/starter.png\n43\tprintf 'load scene=starter\\ninput move=[1,0] ticks=60\\nplay\\ncapture out=out/a.png\\n' | pav repl\n44\tcargo test -p pav_core --test games                    # the starter's tests\n45\tcargo run -p pav_app -- --scene starter                # the window (WASD; needs a display)\n46\t```\n47\t`START_HERE.md` has the Linux packages to install. Look at captures with your image tool;\n48\tsmall sizes are cheaper. With MCP (`.mcp.json`), the same tools are `mcp__shardfall__*`.\n49\t\n50\t**The edit loop.** Running from the repository's root, `pav` reads your level file at every\n51\t`load` (and `play seeds=`), so changes to the map and to its `[game]` numbers need no rebuild.\n52\tA change to Rust code rebuilds `pav` (about 2 minutes on 4 cores) and a test binary (as long\n53\tagain): batch code edits, and put balance numbers in `[game]`.\n54\t\n55\t## Your game in six steps\n56\t1. **Copy the starter.** `crates/pav_core/src/games/starter.rs` and `starter.toml` to\n57\t   `<key>.rs` and `<key>.toml` in the same folder. Rename the struct; set `const KEY` to your\n58\t   key (lowercase, no `/`); point `include_str!` at your level.\n59\t2. **Register it.** In `games/mod.rs`: `pub mod <key>;` and one line in `GAMES`:\n60\t   `entry::<mykey::MyGame>(\"One sentence about it.\", mykey::build)`. Now `load scene=<key>`\n61\t   works in the tools, the window (`--scene <key>`) and the browser (`?scene=<key>`).\n62\t3. **Draw the level** in `<key>.toml` (next section), with your rules' numbers in its `[game]`\n63\t   table (the starter's `Rules`). `pav room_check path=<file>` checks it.\n64\t4. **Write the rules** in your `Game` impl (the hook, below).\n65\t5. **Teach the bot** every rule you add (`Game::bot`): it is how you, the tests and the next\n66\t   agent find out whether the game can be won.\n67\t6. **Copy the tests** in `crates/pav_core/tests/games.rs` to `tests/<key>.rs`: the bot wins on\n68\t   ten seeds, a careless player loses, and snapshots, rewind and replays are exact.\n69\t\n70\t## The level: a room file\n71\tA level is a room file (`rooms/_template.toml` documents every field; the pavilion's\n72\t`rooms/*.toml` are working examples). Rooms built this way stand alone, with no wing or\n73\tcorridor. In a room file:\n74\t- `[layout]`: an ASCII map per floor (`[[layout.layer]]`, 1 cell = 1 m; column = x east,\n75\t  row = z south) and a `legend` per character: floor and wall `blocks` (heights, colours,\n76\t  `destructible`, `crumble`, `ghost`), ladders, `prop`s, `label`s, `grass`, trigger `zone`s\n77\t  (`kill`, `water`, `checkpoint`, `conveyor`, `bounce`, `pad`, `camera`, course\n78\t  `start`/`gate`/`finish`) and `marker = \"<name>\"`.\n79\t- **Markers** are your own pieces: `crate::scenes::markers(sim, \"shard\")` lists every\n80\t  marked cell (world positions, map order). `marker = \"player\"` is the start.\n81\t- `[[npc]]`: characters (`kind = biped | spider | lizard | beetle | blob | quadruped`, `look` colours and\n82\t  proportions, `ai = idle | wander | patrol | circle | follow | guard`). Your game can drive\n83\t  them instead (the starter's slimes).\n84\t- `[[object]]`: library props (`asset = \"LIB/core/<name>\"`; `pav assets find=barrel` searches\n85\t  the store, `assets shelf=prop` lists the best), physics objects, `[[joint]]`s and `[[chain]]`s.\n86\t- `[camera]` (`tilt`, `distance`, `yaw`, `ortho`), `[view]` (light, sky, fog, bloom, styles,\n87\t  filters: `pav params prefix=view` lists them), `[params]` (movement and physics while in\n88\t  the level: `pav params`).\n89\t\n90\t- `[game]`: your rules' numbers, free-form; your `build` reads them (`def.game`).\n91\t- `entrance` is required by the format; with a `player` marker a level doesn't use it.\n92\t\n93\tRead the level with `games::level_text(KEY, include_str!(...))`: the file on disk when running\n94\tfrom the repository, else the copy built in (tests, the browser, an executable). The camera\n95\tfollows the player; to see a whole level from above, `camera preset=top` then\n96\t`set path=camera.distance value=30` (raise it for bigger levels) and `capture`.\n97\t\n98\t## The hook: `pav_core::games::Game`\n99\t```rust\n100\t#[derive(Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n101\tpub struct MyGame { /* the rules' state: score, timers, entity ids, outcome */ }\n102\t\n103\timpl Game for MyGame {\n104\t    const KEY: &'static str = \"mygame\";\n105\t    fn pre(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, input: &InputFrame, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>)\n106\t        -> BTreeMap<EntityId, InputFrame>;          // before movement: inputs for characters you drive\n107\t    fn post(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>); // after physics: rules\n108\t    fn hud(&self, sim: &Sim) -> Vec<String>;        // text lines, top left; `play` returns them\n109\t    fn status(&self, sim: &Sim) -> serde_json::Value; // your state for tools and tests\n110\t    fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome;                   // Playing | Won | Lost\n111\t    fn bot(&self, sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame;         // what a good player would press now\n112\t}\n113\tpub fn build(sim: &mut Sim, rest: Option<&str>) -> Result<()>; // the scene; sets sim.state.play\n114\t```\n115\tEvery method has a default. `build` gets the rest of a scene name `mygame/<rest>` (a level\n116\tnumber, say). Each tick runs: behaviours, your `pre`, characters move (your inputs replace\n117\ttheir own), physics, shots move and hit, your `post`. While `pre`/`post` run, your game is out\n118\tof `sim.state.play`: use `self` for your state and `sim` for the world. In `post`, `events`\n119\talready holds this tick's events: read them to react (`Hit { target, .. }` names the\n120\tcharacter a shot struck: it is stunned, knocked back and safe from shots for 0.6 s), and push\n121\tyour own. From outside, `sim.play()?.get::<MyGame>()` reads your game; in a test,\n122\t`sim.state.play.as_mut().unwrap().get_mut::<MyGame>().unwrap()` changes it.\n123\t\n124\t## Building blocks\n125\t| You need | Use |\n126\t|---|---|\n127\t| The player | `sim.state.player`; it walks by the keys' `InputFrame` (`move_dir`, `buttons::JUMP`, `PRIMARY`...); `sim.feet(id)` is where a character stands (`pos` is its centre) |\n128\t| Characters | `[[npc]]` in the level, or `sim.spawn_npc(name, feet, facing, PuppetDef::preset(BodyPlan::Blob), None, None)`; drive them from `pre` |\n129\t| Pickups, hazards, scenery | `sim.spawn(Spawn::new(\"name\", pos).visual(Visual::new(shape, Color::hex(\"#7fe3ff\"))).behavior(Behavior::Spin { speed }))`; `sim.despawn(id)`; `sim.set_position(id, feet)` |\n130\t| Moving platforms, emitters, spinners | `Behavior::Move`, `Rotate`, `Emitter`, `Spawner` (`entity.rs`), or `[[object]]` in the level |\n131\t| Shots | `sim.state.projectiles.spawn(Projectile { pos, vel, radius, life, color, knockback, gravity, owner, team, damage })`: plain structs, stopped by walls; `Team::Enemy` shots hit characters (a `Hit` event), `Team::Player` shots hit things with `health` |\n132\t| Pathfinding | `nav::NavGrid::for_room(sim)` (the level's walkable grid); `grid.path(from, to)`; `grid.flow(goal, reach)` steers many chasers at once; `grid.walkable(p)` |\n133\t| Randomness | `sim.state.rng` only (`range`, `below`, `f32`): seeded, so a seed always plays the same |\n134\t| Messages | `sim.say(\"text\")`: centre top for a few seconds |\n135\t| Sound and effects | push `SimEvent`s from `pre`/`post`: `Coin`, `Pickup`, `Hit`, `Explosion`, `Shot`, `Jump`, `Land`... The renderer and the synth play them |\n136\t| Tuning | your numbers: the level's `[game]`; the engine's: `pav params`, `pav set path=movement.speed value=4.5` (movement, physics, camera, view), and a level's `[params]` set them while in it |\n137\t| Looks | the level's `[view]`; `pav look` lists looks and filters; `rooms/mix_match.toml` mixes styles per part |\n138\t| Animation | characters animate procedurally; motion clips: `pav assets shelf=clip`, then `clips = [\"SET/Clip\"]` on an `[[npc]]` |\n139\t\n140\t## Rules that keep it working\n141\t- **Same inputs and seed, same game.** Random numbers come from `sim.state.rng`; never read the\n142\t  clock; never let `HashMap`/`HashSet` iteration order reach game state (`BTreeMap`, or sort).\n143\t- **All game state lives in your struct or in entities**, and your struct is `Clone` + serde\n144\t  (snapshots are clones; files and the replay hash are its CBOR). A cache that follows from\n145\t  the level (a nav grid) is `#[serde(skip)]` and rebuilt when missing.\n146\t- **`pav_core` has no window, GPU or audio code**, and game code must run in the browser: no\n147\t  threads, blocking calls or file access (embed data with `include_str!`).\n148\t- **Check with the tools, not by reading engine code.** If a tool or error message didn't tell\n149\t  you what you needed, that is engine feedback.\n150\t\n151\t## Check your work (no screen needed)\n152\t| Question | Command (`pav <tool>` or a `pav repl` line) |\n153\t|---|---|\n154\t| Can it be won? Lost? | `play bot=true seeds=10` (wins, seconds, the state of every run not won) |\n155\t| What's on screen? | `capture width=640 height=360 out=out/a.png`; `filmstrip` for motion in one image; to watch the bot, repeat `play bot=true ticks=30` and `capture` in a `pav repl` |\n156\t| Does a rule fire? | `input move=[1,0] ticks=30`, then `play` (HUD, outcome, `status`) |\n157\t| Where is everything? | `entities`, `player`, `status` (with the state hash) |\n158\t| Same every time? | `record_save path=out/r.json`, then `replay path=out/r.json` (checks the hash); `rewind`, `snapshot_save`/`snapshot_load` |\n159\t| Is the level valid? | `room_check path=crates/pav_core/src/games/<key>.toml` |\n160\t| Does it run fast? | `bench scene=<key> ticks=1200` |\n161\t| Does it still pass? | `cargo test -p pav_core --test <key>`, `cargo clippy -p pav_core --all-targets -- -D warnings`, `cargo fmt --all` |\n162\t\n163\t## Ship it\n164\t- **Window:** `cargo run -p pav_app -- --scene <key>` (Linux; `xvfb-run -a` without a display).\n165\t  F5 restarts the game (Esc opens the menu).\n166\t- **Windows `.exe`:** `scripts/build-windows.sh` (after `scripts/setup-linux.sh`).\n167\t- **Browser, one file:** `scripts/build-web.sh --single-file --scene <key> --title \"My Game\"\n168\t  --out out/mygame.html`: about 10 MB, runs from `file://` in Chrome or Edge (WebGPU).\n169\t\n170\t## Limits today\n171\t- HUD: text lines only (captures draw them; `ui` reads them). Panels, bars or menus mean egui\n172\t  code in `pav_ui` (draw it from `headless.rs` too, so the tools see it).\n173\t- Drawing from game state without an entity (`pav_view::assets::emit_asset`) and new sounds\n174\t  (`pav_audio`, a closed list) mean code outside `pav_core`.\n175\t- Your game compiles into the engine crate (`pav_core/src/games/`), beside Shardfall.\n176\t- The camera follows the player with the level's `[camera]` settings; there is no\n177\t  per-game camera code. Keep levels about 20 m across, or raise `[camera] distance`.\n178\t- The window's controls card lists the engine demo's keys (bombs, vehicles), not your game's.\n179\t\n180\t## Report back\n181\tWhen you hand the game back, add a short **engine feedback** section, written for an engine\n182\tagent who has never seen your work: what you used, the engine source you had to read and\n183\twhy, what you wrote that the engine should have had (with the file), wrong or missing docs,\n184\tand the one change that would have helped most. The maintainers log it in\n185\t`docs/FEEDBACK.md`, and what keeps coming back becomes engine work.\n186\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0691f0d6-f08e-44c9-b7bd-c57d7fb96b71","uuid":"0a41c5ee-95a2-4001-a4da-0bc5fad106f9","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:39:37.395Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223859__x35112d/work/WhiffFit-WiiFit-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-223859/engine/docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md","content":"# Make a game on Pavilion\n\nFor an agent asked to build a game on this engine. Read this page whole (about 3k tokens); it\nis the only one you need to start. Search the others when a step points to them. The engine\nwas made for agents: everything runs, renders and checks headless, and every tool is a CLI\ncommand, a REPL line and an MCP tool.\n\n## What you get\n- **A simulation** on the CPU at 60 ticks a second (`pav_core`): characters that walk, jump,\n  climb and swim, rapier physics, pathfinding, projectiles, zones, procedural animation,\n  props from a library. The same inputs and seed always play the same: snapshots, rewind\n  and replays are exact.\n- **A WebGPU renderer** (`pav_render`, `pav_view`): lighting, shadows, bloom, styles and\n  filters (cel, pixel, painterly...), set per level as data.\n- **Synthesized sound** (`pav_audio`): it plays the simulation's events, no audio files.\n- **Tools** (`pav`): capture a frame, step time, drive the player, run your game's bot over\n  many seeds, record and replay. Images come back as PNGs you can look at.\n- **Three builds** from one source: a window on Linux and Windows, and the browser (also as\n  one self-contained HTML file).\n\nShardfall, the hack-and-slash in `crates/pav_core/src/arpg/`, and the pavilion of demo rooms\nare built on the same engine. They are examples, not starting points: start from the\n**starter game**.\n\n## Read this, skip that\n- **Use:** this page; `crates/pav_core/src/games/` (the hook and the starter, about 600\n  lines); `rooms/_template.toml` (the level format: search it for a field); `docs/REFERENCE.md`\n  \"Tools\" (search for a tool); `pav help`.\n- **`AGENTS.md`** is the rulebook for agents working on the engine and Shardfall. Its\n  \"Key rules\" apply to your game too (below). Its workflow does not: `docs/HISTORY.md`,\n  `docs/PROGRESS.md`, pull requests, escalations and the asset-store plans are the\n  maintainers'. Follow your own task's instructions for commits and delivery.\n- **Skip:** `docs/HISTORY.md`, `docs/ASSET_SYSTEM_PLAN.md`, `docs/AGENT_ASSET_STORE.md`,\n  Shardfall's code and data (`arpg/`, `game/`), `pavilion-lite/` (an older, frozen engine).\n  If you need to read engine source to use the engine, say so in your feedback (below).\n\n## Quick start\n```sh\ncargo build -p pav_tools --bin pav                     # first build: a few minutes\nalias pav=./target/debug/pav\npav play scene=starter bot=true seeds=10               # the starter's bot wins 10/10\npav capture scene=starter ticks=120 width=640 height=360 out=out/starter.png\nprintf 'load scene=starter\\ninput move=[1,0] ticks=60\\nplay\\ncapture out=out/a.png\\n' | pav repl\ncargo test -p pav_core --test games                    # the starter's tests\ncargo run -p pav_app -- --scene starter                # the window (WASD; needs a display)\n```\n`START_HERE.md` has the Linux packages to install. Look at captures with your image tool;\nsmall sizes are cheaper. With MCP (`.mcp.json`), the same tools are `mcp__shardfall__*`.\n\n**The edit loop.** Running from the repository's root, `pav` reads your level file at every\n`load` (and `play seeds=`), so changes to the map and to its `[game]` numbers need no rebuild.\nA change to Rust code rebuilds `pav` (about 2 minutes on 4 cores) and a test binary (as long\nagain): batch code edits, and put balance numbers in `[game]`.\n\n## Your game in six steps\n1. **Copy the starter.** `crates/pav_core/src/games/starter.rs` and `starter.toml` to\n   `<key>.rs` and `<key>.toml` in the same folder. Rename the struct; set `const KEY` to your\n   key (lowercase, no `/`); point `include_str!` at your level.\n2. **Register it.** In `games/mod.rs`: `pub mod <key>;` and one line in `GAMES`:\n   `entry::<mykey::MyGame>(\"One sentence about it.\", mykey::build)`. Now `load scene=<key>`\n   works in the tools, the window (`--scene <key>`) and the browser (`?scene=<key>`).\n3. **Draw the level** in `<key>.toml` (next section), with your rules' numbers in its `[game]`\n   table (the starter's `Rules`). `pav room_check path=<file>` checks it.\n4. **Write the rules** in your `Game` impl (the hook, below).\n5. **Teach the bot** every rule you add (`Game::bot`): it is how you, the tests and the next\n   agent find out whether the game can be won.\n6. **Copy the tests** in `crates/pav_core/tests/games.rs` to `tests/<key>.rs`: the bot wins on\n   ten seeds, a careless player loses, and snapshots, rewind and replays are exact.\n\n## The level: a room file\nA level is a room file (`rooms/_template.toml` documents every field; the pavilion's\n`rooms/*.toml` are working examples). Rooms built this way stand alone, with no wing or\ncorridor. In a room file:\n- `[layout]`: an ASCII map per floor (`[[layout.layer]]`, 1 cell = 1 m; column = x east,\n  row = z south) and a `legend` per character: floor and wall `blocks` (heights, colours,\n  `destructible`, `crumble`, `ghost`), ladders, `prop`s, `label`s, `grass`, trigger `zone`s\n  (`kill`, `water`, `checkpoint`, `conveyor`, `bounce`, `pad`, `camera`, course\n  `start`/`gate`/`finish`) and `marker = \"<name>\"`.\n- **Markers** are your own pieces: `crate::scenes::markers(sim, \"shard\")` lists every\n  marked cell (world positions, map order). `marker = \"player\"` is the start.\n- `[[npc]]`: characters (`kind = biped | spider | lizard | beetle | blob | quadruped`, `look` colours and\n  proportions, `ai = idle | wander | patrol | circle | follow | guard`). Your game can drive\n  them instead (the starter's slimes).\n- `[[object]]`: library props (`asset = \"LIB/core/<name>\"`; `pav assets find=barrel` searches\n  the store, `assets shelf=prop` lists the best), physics objects, `[[joint]]`s and `[[chain]]`s.\n- `[camera]` (`tilt`, `distance`, `yaw`, `ortho`), `[view]` (light, sky, fog, bloom, styles,\n  filters: `pav params prefix=view` lists them), `[params]` (movement and physics while in\n  the level: `pav params`).\n\n- `[game]`: your rules' numbers, free-form; your `build` reads them (`def.game`).\n- `entrance` is required by the format; with a `player` marker a level doesn't use it.\n\nRead the level with `games::level_text(KEY, include_str!(...))`: the file on disk when running\nfrom the repository, else the copy built in (tests, the browser, an executable). The camera\nfollows the player; to see a whole level from above, `camera preset=top` then\n`set path=camera.distance value=30` (raise it for bigger levels) and `capture`.\n\n## The hook: `pav_core::games::Game`\n```rust\n#[derive(Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct MyGame { /* the rules' state: score, timers, entity ids, outcome */ }\n\nimpl Game for MyGame {\n    const KEY: &'static str = \"mygame\";\n    fn pre(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, input: &InputFrame, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>)\n        -> BTreeMap<EntityId, InputFrame>;          // before movement: inputs for characters you drive\n    fn post(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>); // after physics: rules\n    fn hud(&self, sim: &Sim) -> Vec<String>;        // text lines, top left; `play` returns them\n    fn status(&self, sim: &Sim) -> serde_json::Value; // your state for tools and tests\n    fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome;                   // Playing | Won | Lost\n    fn bot(&self, sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame;         // what a good player would press now\n}\npub fn build(sim: &mut Sim, rest: Option<&str>) -> Result<()>; // the scene; sets sim.state.play\n```\nEvery method has a default. `build` gets the rest of a scene name `mygame/<rest>` (a level\nnumber, say). Each tick runs: behaviours, your `pre`, characters move (your inputs replace\ntheir own), physics, shots move and hit, your `post`. While `pre`/`post` run, your game is out\nof `sim.state.play`: use `self` for your state and `sim` for the world. In `post`, `events`\nalready holds this tick's events: read them to react (`Hit { target, .. }` names the\ncharacter a shot struck: it is stunned, knocked back and safe from shots for 0.6 s), and push\nyour own. From outside, `sim.play()?.get::<MyGame>()` reads your game; in a test,\n`sim.state.play.as_mut().unwrap().get_mut::<MyGame>().unwrap()` changes it.\n\n## Building blocks\n| You need | Use |\n|---|---|\n| The player | `sim.state.player`; it walks by the keys' `InputFrame` (`move_dir`, `buttons::JUMP`, `PRIMARY`...); `sim.feet(id)` is where a character stands (`pos` is its centre) |\n| Characters | `[[npc]]` in the level, or `sim.spawn_npc(name, feet, facing, PuppetDef::preset(BodyPlan::Blob), None, None)`; drive them from `pre` |\n| Pickups, hazards, scenery | `sim.spawn(Spawn::new(\"name\", pos).visual(Visual::new(shape, Color::hex(\"#7fe3ff\"))).behavior(Behavior::Spin { speed }))`; `sim.despawn(id)`; `sim.set_position(id, feet)` |\n| Moving platforms, emitters, spinners | `Behavior::Move`, `Rotate`, `Emitter`, `Spawner` (`entity.rs`), or `[[object]]` in the level |\n| Shots | `sim.state.projectiles.spawn(Projectile { pos, vel, radius, life, color, knockback, gravity, owner, team, damage })`: plain structs, stopped by walls; `Team::Enemy` shots hit characters (a `Hit` event), `Team::Player` shots hit things with `health` |\n| Pathfinding | `nav::NavGrid::for_room(sim)` (the level's walkable grid); `grid.path(from, to)`; `grid.flow(goal, reach)` steers many chasers at once; `grid.walkable(p)` |\n| Randomness | `sim.state.rng` only (`range`, `below`, `f32`): seeded, so a seed always plays the same |\n| Messages | `sim.say(\"text\")`: centre top for a few seconds |\n| Sound and effects | push `SimEvent`s from `pre`/`post`: `Coin`, `Pickup`, `Hit`, `Explosion`, `Shot`, `Jump`, `Land`... The renderer and the synth play them |\n| Tuning | your numbers: the level's `[game]`; the engine's: `pav params`, `pav set path=movement.speed value=4.5` (movement, physics, camera, view), and a level's `[params]` set them while in it |\n| Looks | the level's `[view]`; `pav look` lists looks and filters; `rooms/mix_match.toml` mixes styles per part |\n| Animation | characters animate procedurally; motion clips: `pav assets shelf=clip`, then `clips = [\"SET/Clip\"]` on an `[[npc]]` |\n\n## Rules that keep it working\n- **Same inputs and seed, same game.** Random numbers come from `sim.state.rng`; never read the\n  clock; never let `HashMap`/`HashSet` iteration order reach game state (`BTreeMap`, or sort).\n- **All game state lives in your struct or in entities**, and your struct is `Clone` + serde\n  (snapshots are clones; files and the replay hash are its CBOR). A cache that follows from\n  the level (a nav grid) is `#[serde(skip)]` and rebuilt when missing.\n- **`pav_core` has no window, GPU or audio code**, and game code must run in the browser: no\n  threads, blocking calls or file access (embed data with `include_str!`).\n- **Check with the tools, not by reading engine code.** If a tool or error message didn't tell\n  you what you needed, that is engine feedback.\n\n## Check your work (no screen needed)\n| Question | Command (`pav <tool>` or a `pav repl` line) |\n|---|---|\n| Can it be won? Lost? | `play bot=true seeds=10` (wins, seconds, the state of every run not won) |\n| What's on screen? | `capture width=640 height=360 out=out/a.png`; `filmstrip` for motion in one image; to watch the bot, repeat `play bot=true ticks=30` and `capture` in a `pav repl` |\n| Does a rule fire? | `input move=[1,0] ticks=30`, then `play` (HUD, outcome, `status`) |\n| Where is everything? | `entities`, `player`, `status` (with the state hash) |\n| Same every time? | `record_save path=out/r.json`, then `replay path=out/r.json` (checks the hash); `rewind`, `snapshot_save`/`snapshot_load` |\n| Is the level valid? | `room_check path=crates/pav_core/src/games/<key>.toml` |\n| Does it run fast? | `bench scene=<key> ticks=1200` |\n| Does it still pass? | `cargo test -p pav_core --test <key>`, `cargo clippy -p pav_core --all-targets -- -D warnings`, `cargo fmt --all` |\n\n## Ship it\n- **Window:** `cargo run -p pav_app -- --scene <key>` (Linux; `xvfb-run -a` without a display).\n  F5 restarts the game (Esc opens the menu).\n- **Windows `.exe`:** `scripts/build-windows.sh` (after `scripts/setup-linux.sh`).\n- **Browser, one file:** `scripts/build-web.sh --single-file --scene <key> --title \"My Game\"\n  --out out/mygame.html`: about 10 MB, runs from `file://` in Chrome or Edge (WebGPU).\n\n## Limits today\n- HUD: text lines only (captures draw them; `ui` reads them). Panels, bars or menus mean egui\n  code in `pav_ui` (draw it from `headless.rs` too, so the tools see it).\n- Drawing from game state without an entity (`pav_view::assets::emit_asset`) and new sounds\n  (`pav_audio`, a closed list) mean code outside `pav_core`.\n- Your game compiles into the engine crate (`pav_core/src/games/`), beside Shardfall.\n- The camera follows the player with the level's `[camera]` settings; there is no\n  per-game camera code. Keep levels about 20 m across, or raise `[camera] distance`.\n- The window's controls card lists the engine demo's keys (bombs, vehicles), not your game's.\n\n## Report back\nWhen you hand the game back, add a short **engine feedback** section, written for an engine\nagent who has never seen your work: what you used, the engine source you had to read and\nwhy, what you wrote that the engine should have had (with the file), wrong or missing docs,\nand the one change that would have helped most. The maintainers log it in\n`docs/FEEDBACK.md`, and what keeps coming back becomes engine work.\n","numLines":186,"startLine":1,"totalLines":186}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_016eNeYkZBwnuGJbxroL2GpQ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjF69T2LENYS88ZNRGww","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017xhF9WcJkRYjR6tSrteBvq","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223859__x35112d/work/WhiffFit-WiiFit-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-223859/engine/crates/pav_core/src/games/starter.rs"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1331,"cache_read_input_tokens":55433,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1331},"output_tokens":21,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0691f0d6-f08e-44c9-b7bd-c57d7fb96b71","uuid":"0edb9a49-02b8-4e6d-a198-97e8818ff0f0","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:39:38.431Z","request_id":"req_011CgjF69DdUuAy4yo1tr6B1","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_017xhF9WcJkRYjR6tSrteBvq":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223859__x35112d/work/WhiffFit-WiiFit-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-223859/engine/crates/pav_core/src/games/starter.rs"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017xhF9WcJkRYjR6tSrteBvq","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! The starter game, and the template for a new one: collect every shard while slimes chase\n2\t//! you round the walls. Three lives; a slime's touch sends everyone back to the start.\n3\t//!\n4\t//! To start a game of your own, copy this file and `starter.toml` to `<key>.rs` and\n5\t//! `<key>.toml`, change `KEY`, add its line to `GAMES` in `mod.rs`, then change the rules.\n6\t//! `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md` walks through it.\n7\t\n8\tuse std::collections::BTreeMap;\n9\tuse std::sync::Arc;\n10\t\n11\tuse anyhow::{Result, anyhow};\n12\tuse glam::{Vec2, Vec3};\n13\tuse serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};\n14\tuse serde_json::json;\n15\t\n16\tuse super::{Game, GameBox, Outcome};\n17\tuse crate::color::Color;\n18\tuse crate::entity::{Behavior, EntityId, Spawn};\n19\tuse crate::frame::SimEvent;\n20\tuse crate::input::InputFrame;\n21\tuse crate::nav::NavGrid;\n22\tuse crate::room::RoomDef;\n23\tuse crate::shape::{Shape, Visual};\n24\tuse crate::sim::Sim;\n25\t\n26\t/// The level, a room file, built in (the browser has no files). Running from the repository,\n27\t/// `level_text` reads the file on disk instead, so an edit shows at the next `load`.\n28\tconst LEVEL: &str = include_str!(\"starter.toml\");\n29\t\n30\t/// The rules' numbers: the level's `[game]` table, each with a default, so balancing is an\n31\t/// edit to the level and a `load` (no rebuild).\n32\t#[derive(Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n33\t#[serde(default, deny_unknown_fields)]\n34\tpub struct Rules {\n35\t    pub lives: u32,\n36\t    /// Slime speed as a fraction of a walker's top speed (each slime gets ±15%, from the seed).\n37\t    pub slime_speed: f32,\n38\t    /// Slimes chase a player within this walking distance (m).\n39\t    pub slime_sight: f32,\n40\t    /// Feet closer than this (m) catch the player.\n41\t    pub catch: f32,\n42\t    /// Feet closer than this (m) to a shard pick it up.\n43\t    pub pick: f32,\n44\t    /// Seconds the player is safe at the start and after a catch.\n45\t    pub grace: f32,\n46\t}\n47\t\n48\timpl Default for Rules {\n49\t    fn default() -> Self {\n50\t        Self { lives: 3, slime_speed: 0.5, slime_sight: 9.0, catch: 0.8, pick: 0.9, grace: 2.0 }\n51\t    }\n52\t}\n53\t\n54\t#[derive(Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n55\tpub struct Starter {\n56\t    pub rules: Rules,\n57\t    /// Shards still on the floor.\n58\t    pub shards: Vec<EntityId>,\n59\t    pub total: usize,\n60\t    pub slimes: Vec<EntityId>,\n61\t    /// Each slime's start (feet) and speed.\n62\t    pub homes: Vec<Vec3>,\n63\t    pub speeds: Vec<f32>,\n64\t    pub lives: u32,\n65\t    /// Seconds the player is still safe from slimes.\n66\t    pub grace: f32,\n67\t    pub time: f32,\n68\t    pub outcome: Outcome,\n69\t    /// The walkable grid. It follows from the level alone, so it isn't saved: rebuilt when\n70\t    /// missing (after a snapshot load).\n71\t    #[serde(skip)]\n72\t    nav: Option<Arc<NavGrid>>,\n73\t}\n74\t\n75\t/// Builds the level, places the shards on its `shard` markers and starts the game.\n76\tpub fn build(sim: &mut Sim, _rest: Option<&str>) -> Result<()> {\n77\t    let def = RoomDef::parse(&super::level_text(Starter::KEY, LEVEL)).map_err(|e| anyhow!(\"starter.toml: {e}\"))?;\n78\t    let rules: Rules = toml::Value::Table(def.game.clone()).try_into().map_err(|e| anyhow!(\"starter.toml [game]: {e}\"))?;\n79\t    crate::scenes::build_standalone_room(sim, Starter::KEY, def);\n80\t    let mut shards = Vec::new();\n81\t    for at in crate::scenes::markers(sim, \"shard\") {\n82\t        let mut look = Visual::new(Shape::Box { half: Vec3::splat(0.2) }, Color::hex(\"#7fe3ff\"));\n83\t        look.emissive = 0.8;\n84\t        let shard = Spawn::new(\"shard\", at + Vec3::Y * 0.7).visual(look).behavior(Behavior::Spin { speed: 2.5 });\n85\t        shards.push(sim.spawn(shard));\n86\t    }\n87\t    // Slimes come from the level's [[npc]] entries; the game drives them.\n88\t    let slimes: Vec<EntityId> = sim.state.entities.iter().filter(|e| e.name == \"slime\").map(|e| e.id).collect();\n89\t    let homes = slimes.iter().filter_map(|&id| sim.feet(id)).collect();\n90\t    // Randomness comes from the sim's seeded generator, so a seed always plays the same.\n91\t    let speeds = slimes.iter().map(|_| rules.slime_speed * sim.state.rng.range(0.85, 1.15)).collect();\n92\t    let game = Starter {\n93\t        total: shards.len(),\n94\t        shards,\n95\t        slimes,\n96\t        homes,\n97\t        speeds,\n98\t        lives: rules.lives,\n99\t        grace: rules.grace,\n100\t        rules,\n101\t        time: 0.0,\n102\t        outcome: Outcome::Playing,\n103\t        nav: None,\n104\t    };\n105\t    sim.state.play = Some(GameBox::new(game));\n106\t    Ok(())\n107\t}\n108\t\n109\timpl Game for Starter {\n110\t    const KEY: &'static str = \"starter\";\n111\t\n112\t    fn pre(\n113\t        &mut self,\n114\t        sim: &mut Sim,\n115\t        _input: &InputFrame,\n116\t        _dt: f32,\n117\t        _events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>,\n118\t    ) -> BTreeMap<EntityId, InputFrame> {\n119\t        let mut out = BTreeMap::new();\n120\t        if self.outcome != Outcome::Playing {\n121\t            // Decided: everyone stands still.\n122\t            for &id in sim.state.player.iter().chain(&self.slimes) {\n123\t                out.insert(id, InputFrame::default());\n124\t            }\n125\t            return out;\n126\t        }\n127\t        if self.nav.is_none() {\n128\t            self.nav = NavGrid::for_room(sim).map(Arc::new);\n129\t        }\n130\t        let Some(nav) = self.nav.clone() else { return out };\n131\t        // One flow field toward the player steers every slime round the walls.\n132\t        let player = sim.state.player.and_then(|id| sim.feet(id));\n133\t        let field = player.filter(|_| self.grace <= 0.0).and_then(|p| nav.flow(p, self.rules.slime_sight));\n134\t        for (&id, &speed) in self.slimes.iter().zip(&self.speeds) {\n135\t            let mut input = InputFrame::default();\n136\t            if let (Some(field), Some(at)) = (&field, sim.feet(id))\n137\t                && let Some(d) = field.dir(&nav, at)\n138\t            {\n139\t                input.move_dir = Vec2::new(d.x, d.z) * speed;\n140\t            }\n141\t            out.insert(id, input);\n142\t        }\n143\t        out\n144\t    }\n145\t\n146\t    fn post(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>) {\n147\t        if self.outcome != Outcome::Playing {\n148\t            return;\n149\t        }\n150\t        self.time += dt;\n151\t        self.grace = (self.grace - dt).max(0.0);\n152\t        let Some(pid) = sim.state.player else { return };\n153\t        let Some(p) = sim.feet(pid) else { return };\n154\t\n155\t        // Shards: walk over one to pick it up.\n156\t        let near: Vec<(EntityId, Vec3)> = self\n157\t            .shards\n158\t            .iter()\n159\t            .filter_map(|&id| sim.state.entities.get(id).map(|e| (id, e.pos)))\n160\t            .filter(|(_, at)| flat(*at - p) < self.rules.pick)\n161\t            .collect();\n162\t        for (id, at) in near {\n163\t            self.shards.retain(|&s| s != id);\n164\t            sim.despawn(id);\n165\t            events.push(SimEvent::Coin { pos: at });\n166\t            let left = self.shards.len();\n167\t            sim.say(if left == 1 { \"1 shard to go\".to_string() } else { format!(\"{left} shards to go\") });\n168\t        }\n169\t        if self.shards.is_empty() {\n170\t            self.outcome = Outcome::Won;\n171\t            sim.say(format!(\"All shards in {:.1} s!\", self.time));\n172\t            return;\n173\t        }\n174\t\n175\t        // Slimes: a touch costs a life and sends everyone back to the start.\n176\t        let catch = self.rules.catch;\n177\t        let caught = self.grace <= 0.0 && self.slimes.iter().any(|&id| sim.feet(id).is_some_and(|s| flat(s - p) < catch));\n178\t        if caught {\n179\t            self.lives -= 1;\n180\t            events.push(SimEvent::Hit { pos: p + Vec3::Y, strength: 1.0, target: Some(pid) });\n181\t            if self.lives == 0 {\n182\t                self.outcome = Outcome::Lost;\n183\t                sim.say(\"Caught! Game over\");\n184\t                return;\n185\t            }\n186\t            sim.say(format!(\"Caught! {} lives left\", self.lives));\n187\t            let spawn = sim.state.spawn;\n188\t            sim.set_position(pid, spawn);\n189\t            for (&id, &home) in self.slimes.iter().zip(&self.homes) {\n190\t                sim.set_position(id, home);\n191\t            }\n192\t            self.grace = self.rules.grace;\n193\t        }\n194\t    }\n195\t\n196\t    fn hud(&self, _sim: &Sim) -> Vec<String> {\n197\t        let mut lines =\n198\t            vec![format!(\"Shards {}/{}\", self.total - self.shards.len(), self.total), format!(\"Lives {}\", self.lives)];\n199\t        match self.outcome {\n200\t            Outcome::Won => lines.push(format!(\"You win! {:.1} s\", self.time)),\n201\t            Outcome::Lost => lines.push(\"Game over\".into()),\n202\t            Outcome::Playing => {}\n203\t        }\n204\t        lines\n205\t    }\n206\t\n207\t    fn status(&self, sim: &Sim) -> serde_json::Value {\n208\t        let xz = |id: EntityId| sim.feet(id).map(|f| [round(f.x), round(f.z)]);\n209\t        json!({\n210\t            \"outcome\": self.outcome,\n211\t            \"time\": round(self.time),\n212\t            \"shards_left\": self.shards.len(),\n213\t            \"total\": self.total,\n214\t            \"lives\": self.lives,\n215\t            \"grace\": round(self.grace),\n216\t            \"player\": sim.state.player.and_then(xz),\n217\t            \"shards\": self.shards.iter().filter_map(|&id| sim.state.entities.get(id).map(|e| [round(e.pos.x), round(e.pos.z)])).collect::<Vec<_>>(),\n218\t            \"slimes\": self.slimes.iter().filter_map(|&id| xz(id)).collect::<Vec<_>>(),\n219\t        })\n220\t    }\n221\t\n222\t    fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome {\n223\t        self.outcome\n224\t    }\n225\t\n226\t    /// Takes the shortest walk to a shard that keeps clear of slimes (cells near them count\n227\t    /// as walls); with every shard cut off, makes for the reachable spot farthest from them.\n228\t    fn bot(&self, sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame {\n229\t        let mut input = InputFrame::default();\n230\t        let Some(p) = sim.state.player.and_then(|id| sim.feet(id)) else { return input };\n231\t        let Some(mut grid) = self.nav.as_deref().cloned().or_else(|| NavGrid::for_room(sim)) else { return input };\n232\t        let slimes: Vec<Vec3> = self.slimes.iter().filter_map(|&id| sim.feet(id)).collect();\n233\t        if self.grace < 0.5 {\n234\t            for i in 0..grid.blocked.len() {\n235\t                if slimes.iter().any(|&s| flat(grid.center(i) - s) < 1.8) {\n236\t                    grid.blocked[i] = true;\n237\t                }\n238\t            }\n239\t        }\n240\t        let paths = self.shards.iter().filter_map(|&id| sim.state.entities.get(id)).filter_map(|e| grid.path(p, e.pos));\n241\t        let path = paths.min_by(|a, b| length(p, a).total_cmp(&length(p, b))).or_else(|| {\n242\t            let clearance = |i: usize| slimes.iter().map(|&s| flat(grid.center(i) - s)).fold(f32::MAX, f32::min);\n243\t            let reach = grid.flow(p, 6.0)?;\n244\t            let safest = (0..grid.blocked.len())\n245\t                .filter(|&i| reach.dist[i].is_finite())\n246\t                .max_by(|&a, &b| clearance(a).total_cmp(&clearance(b)))?;\n247\t            grid.path(p, grid.center(safest))\n248\t        });\n249\t        input.move_dir = path.map(|path| flat_dir(path[0] - p)).unwrap_or_default();\n250\t        input\n251\t    }\n252\t}\n253\t\n254\tfn flat(v: Vec3) -> f32 {\n255\t    Vec2::new(v.x, v.z).length()\n256\t}\n257\t\n258\tfn flat_dir(v: Vec3) -> Vec2 {\n259\t    Vec2::new(v.x, v.z).normalize_or_zero()\n260\t}\n261\t\n262\t/// Walking length of a path from `p`.\n263\tfn length(p: Vec3, path: &[Vec3]) -> f32 {\n264\t    let (mut at, mut sum) = (p, 0.0);\n265\t    for &q in path {\n266\t        sum += flat(q - at);\n267\t        at = q;\n268\t    }\n269\t    sum\n270\t}\n271\t\n272\tfn round(x: f32) -> f64 {\n273\t    (x as f64 * 100.0).round() / 100.0\n274\t}\n275\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0691f0d6-f08e-44c9-b7bd-c57d7fb96b71","uuid":"586bfe80-13eb-4d52-848e-e190585bcbbd","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:39:38.445Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223859__x35112d/work/WhiffFit-WiiFit-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-223859/engine/crates/pav_core/src/games/starter.rs","content":"//! The starter game, and the template for a new one: collect every shard while slimes chase\n//! you round the walls. Three lives; a slime's touch sends everyone back to the start.\n//!\n//! To start a game of your own, copy this file and `starter.toml` to `<key>.rs` and\n//! `<key>.toml`, change `KEY`, add its line to `GAMES` in `mod.rs`, then change the rules.\n//! `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md` walks through it.\n\nuse std::collections::BTreeMap;\nuse std::sync::Arc;\n\nuse anyhow::{Result, anyhow};\nuse glam::{Vec2, Vec3};\nuse serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};\nuse serde_json::json;\n\nuse super::{Game, GameBox, Outcome};\nuse crate::color::Color;\nuse crate::entity::{Behavior, EntityId, Spawn};\nuse crate::frame::SimEvent;\nuse crate::input::InputFrame;\nuse crate::nav::NavGrid;\nuse crate::room::RoomDef;\nuse crate::shape::{Shape, Visual};\nuse crate::sim::Sim;\n\n/// The level, a room file, built in (the browser has no files). Running from the repository,\n/// `level_text` reads the file on disk instead, so an edit shows at the next `load`.\nconst LEVEL: &str = include_str!(\"starter.toml\");\n\n/// The rules' numbers: the level's `[game]` table, each with a default, so balancing is an\n/// edit to the level and a `load` (no rebuild).\n#[derive(Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n#[serde(default, deny_unknown_fields)]\npub struct Rules {\n    pub lives: u32,\n    /// Slime speed as a fraction of a walker's top speed (each slime gets ±15%, from the seed).\n    pub slime_speed: f32,\n    /// Slimes chase a player within this walking distance (m).\n    pub slime_sight: f32,\n    /// Feet closer than this (m) catch the player.\n    pub catch: f32,\n    /// Feet closer than this (m) to a shard pick it up.\n    pub pick: f32,\n    /// Seconds the player is safe at the start and after a catch.\n    pub grace: f32,\n}\n\nimpl Default for Rules {\n    fn default() -> Self {\n        Self { lives: 3, slime_speed: 0.5, slime_sight: 9.0, catch: 0.8, pick: 0.9, grace: 2.0 }\n    }\n}\n\n#[derive(Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct Starter {\n    pub rules: Rules,\n    /// Shards still on the floor.\n    pub shards: Vec<EntityId>,\n    pub total: usize,\n    pub slimes: Vec<EntityId>,\n    /// Each slime's start (feet) and speed.\n    pub homes: Vec<Vec3>,\n    pub speeds: Vec<f32>,\n    pub lives: u32,\n    /// Seconds the player is still safe from slimes.\n    pub grace: f32,\n    pub time: f32,\n    pub outcome: Outcome,\n    /// The walkable grid. It follows from the level alone, so it isn't saved: rebuilt when\n    /// missing (after a snapshot load).\n    #[serde(skip)]\n    nav: Option<Arc<NavGrid>>,\n}\n\n/// Builds the level, places the shards on its `shard` markers and starts the game.\npub fn build(sim: &mut Sim, _rest: Option<&str>) -> Result<()> {\n    let def = RoomDef::parse(&super::level_text(Starter::KEY, LEVEL)).map_err(|e| anyhow!(\"starter.toml: {e}\"))?;\n    let rules: Rules = toml::Value::Table(def.game.clone()).try_into().map_err(|e| anyhow!(\"starter.toml [game]: {e}\"))?;\n    crate::scenes::build_standalone_room(sim, Starter::KEY, def);\n    let mut shards = Vec::new();\n    for at in crate::scenes::markers(sim, \"shard\") {\n        let mut look = Visual::new(Shape::Box { half: Vec3::splat(0.2) }, Color::hex(\"#7fe3ff\"));\n        look.emissive = 0.8;\n        let shard = Spawn::new(\"shard\", at + Vec3::Y * 0.7).visual(look).behavior(Behavior::Spin { speed: 2.5 });\n        shards.push(sim.spawn(shard));\n    }\n    // Slimes come from the level's [[npc]] entries; the game drives them.\n    let slimes: Vec<EntityId> = sim.state.entities.iter().filter(|e| e.name == \"slime\").map(|e| e.id).collect();\n    let homes = slimes.iter().filter_map(|&id| sim.feet(id)).collect();\n    // Randomness comes from the sim's seeded generator, so a seed always plays the same.\n    let speeds = slimes.iter().map(|_| rules.slime_speed * sim.state.rng.range(0.85, 1.15)).collect();\n    let game = Starter {\n        total: shards.len(),\n        shards,\n        slimes,\n        homes,\n        speeds,\n        lives: rules.lives,\n        grace: rules.grace,\n        rules,\n        time: 0.0,\n        outcome: Outcome::Playing,\n        nav: None,\n    };\n    sim.state.play = Some(GameBox::new(game));\n    Ok(())\n}\n\nimpl Game for Starter {\n    const KEY: &'static str = \"starter\";\n\n    fn pre(\n        &mut self,\n        sim: &mut Sim,\n        _input: &InputFrame,\n        _dt: f32,\n        _events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>,\n    ) -> BTreeMap<EntityId, InputFrame> {\n        let mut out = BTreeMap::new();\n        if self.outcome != Outcome::Playing {\n            // Decided: everyone stands still.\n            for &id in sim.state.player.iter().chain(&self.slimes) {\n                out.insert(id, InputFrame::default());\n            }\n            return out;\n        }\n        if self.nav.is_none() {\n            self.nav = NavGrid::for_room(sim).map(Arc::new);\n        }\n        let Some(nav) = self.nav.clone() else { return out };\n        // One flow field toward the player steers every slime round the walls.\n        let player = sim.state.player.and_then(|id| sim.feet(id));\n        let field = player.filter(|_| self.grace <= 0.0).and_then(|p| nav.flow(p, self.rules.slime_sight));\n        for (&id, &speed) in self.slimes.iter().zip(&self.speeds) {\n            let mut input = InputFrame::default();\n            if let (Some(field), Some(at)) = (&field, sim.feet(id))\n                && let Some(d) = field.dir(&nav, at)\n            {\n                input.move_dir = Vec2::new(d.x, d.z) * speed;\n            }\n            out.insert(id, input);\n        }\n        out\n    }\n\n    fn post(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>) {\n        if self.outcome != Outcome::Playing {\n            return;\n        }\n        self.time += dt;\n        self.grace = (self.grace - dt).max(0.0);\n        let Some(pid) = sim.state.player else { return };\n        let Some(p) = sim.feet(pid) else { return };\n\n        // Shards: walk over one to pick it up.\n        let near: Vec<(EntityId, Vec3)> = self\n            .shards\n            .iter()\n            .filter_map(|&id| sim.state.entities.get(id).map(|e| (id, e.pos)))\n            .filter(|(_, at)| flat(*at - p) < self.rules.pick)\n            .collect();\n        for (id, at) in near {\n            self.shards.retain(|&s| s != id);\n            sim.despawn(id);\n            events.push(SimEvent::Coin { pos: at });\n            let left = self.shards.len();\n            sim.say(if left == 1 { \"1 shard to go\".to_string() } else { format!(\"{left} shards to go\") });\n        }\n        if self.shards.is_empty() {\n            self.outcome = Outcome::Won;\n            sim.say(format!(\"All shards in {:.1} s!\", self.time));\n            return;\n        }\n\n        // Slimes: a touch costs a life and sends everyone back to the start.\n        let catch = self.rules.catch;\n        let caught = self.grace <= 0.0 && self.slimes.iter().any(|&id| sim.feet(id).is_some_and(|s| flat(s - p) < catch));\n        if caught {\n            self.lives -= 1;\n            events.push(SimEvent::Hit { pos: p + Vec3::Y, strength: 1.0, target: Some(pid) });\n            if self.lives == 0 {\n                self.outcome = Outcome::Lost;\n                sim.say(\"Caught! Game over\");\n                return;\n            }\n            sim.say(format!(\"Caught! {} lives left\", self.lives));\n            let spawn = sim.state.spawn;\n            sim.set_position(pid, spawn);\n            for (&id, &home) in self.slimes.iter().zip(&self.homes) {\n                sim.set_position(id, home);\n            }\n            self.grace = self.rules.grace;\n        }\n    }\n\n    fn hud(&self, _sim: &Sim) -> Vec<String> {\n        let mut lines =\n            vec![format!(\"Shards {}/{}\", self.total - self.shards.len(), self.total), format!(\"Lives {}\", self.lives)];\n        match self.outcome {\n            Outcome::Won => lines.push(format!(\"You win! {:.1} s\", self.time)),\n            Outcome::Lost => lines.push(\"Game over\".into()),\n            Outcome::Playing => {}\n        }\n        lines\n    }\n\n    fn status(&self, sim: &Sim) -> serde_json::Value {\n        let xz = |id: EntityId| sim.feet(id).map(|f| [round(f.x), round(f.z)]);\n        json!({\n            \"outcome\": self.outcome,\n            \"time\": round(self.time),\n            \"shards_left\": self.shards.len(),\n            \"total\": self.total,\n            \"lives\": self.lives,\n            \"grace\": round(self.grace),\n            \"player\": sim.state.player.and_then(xz),\n            \"shards\": self.shards.iter().filter_map(|&id| sim.state.entities.get(id).map(|e| [round(e.pos.x), round(e.pos.z)])).collect::<Vec<_>>(),\n            \"slimes\": self.slimes.iter().filter_map(|&id| xz(id)).collect::<Vec<_>>(),\n        })\n    }\n\n    fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome {\n        self.outcome\n    }\n\n    /// Takes the shortest walk to a shard that keeps clear of slimes (cells near them count\n    /// as walls); with every shard cut off, makes for the reachable spot farthest from them.\n    fn bot(&self, sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame {\n        let mut input = InputFrame::default();\n        let Some(p) = sim.state.player.and_then(|id| sim.feet(id)) else { return input };\n        let Some(mut grid) = self.nav.as_deref().cloned().or_else(|| NavGrid::for_room(sim)) else { return input };\n        let slimes: Vec<Vec3> = self.slimes.iter().filter_map(|&id| sim.feet(id)).collect();\n        if self.grace < 0.5 {\n            for i in 0..grid.blocked.len() {\n                if slimes.iter().any(|&s| flat(grid.center(i) - s) < 1.8) {\n                    grid.blocked[i] = true;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        let paths = self.shards.iter().filter_map(|&id| sim.state.entities.get(id)).filter_map(|e| grid.path(p, e.pos));\n        let path = paths.min_by(|a, b| length(p, a).total_cmp(&length(p, b))).or_else(|| {\n            let clearance = |i: usize| slimes.iter().map(|&s| flat(grid.center(i) - s)).fold(f32::MAX, f32::min);\n            let reach = grid.flow(p, 6.0)?;\n            let safest = (0..grid.blocked.len())\n                .filter(|&i| reach.dist[i].is_finite())\n                .max_by(|&a, &b| clearance(a).total_cmp(&clearance(b)))?;\n            grid.path(p, grid.center(safest))\n        });\n        input.move_dir = path.map(|path| flat_dir(path[0] - p)).unwrap_or_default();\n        input\n    }\n}\n\nfn flat(v: Vec3) -> f32 {\n    Vec2::new(v.x, v.z).length()\n}\n\nfn flat_dir(v: Vec3) -> Vec2 {\n    Vec2::new(v.x, v.z).normalize_or_zero()\n}\n\n/// Walking length of a path from `p`.\nfn length(p: Vec3, path: &[Vec3]) -> f32 {\n    let (mut at, mut sum) = (p, 0.0);\n    for &q in path {\n        sum += flat(q - at);\n        at = q;\n    }\n    sum\n}\n\nfn round(x: f32) -> f64 {\n    (x as f64 * 100.0).round() / 100.0\n}\n","numLines":275,"startLine":1,"totalLines":275}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_017xhF9WcJkRYjR6tSrteBvq","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjF69T2LENYS88ZNRGww","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01W91S4BkBp3Asoqqb8sKwAY","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223859__x35112d/work/WhiffFit-WiiFit-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-223859/engine/START_HERE.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1331,"cache_read_input_tokens":55433,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1331},"output_tokens":21,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0691f0d6-f08e-44c9-b7bd-c57d7fb96b71","uuid":"c7c2d808-e66e-43fc-bdb1-a739a40d020d","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:39:39.323Z","request_id":"req_011CgjF69DdUuAy4yo1tr6B1","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01W91S4BkBp3Asoqqb8sKwAY":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223859__x35112d/work/WhiffFit-WiiFit-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-223859/engine/START_HERE.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01W91S4BkBp3Asoqqb8sKwAY","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Shardfall and the Pavilion engine: start here\n2\t\n3\tA Rust game engine built for AI agents (Pavilion), the hack-and-slash built on it (Shardfall)\n4\tand a pavilion of demo rooms. This page is for an agent receiving the source, from a clone of\n5\thttps://github.com/michaelcrosato/shardfall or from a handoff archive (no Git history, build\n6\tcaches, saved heroes or executables; `git init` if you want to track your changes).\n7\t\n8\t## Which route\n9\t- **Make a new game on the engine:** read `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md` whole. It is the only page\n10\t  you need to start; it begins from the starter game (`crates/pav_core/src/games/`).\n11\t- **Work on the engine or on Shardfall:** read `docs/DOCTRINE.md`, `AGENTS.md` and\n12\t  `docs/PROGRESS.md` (Claude Code loads them through `CLAUDE.md`); `docs/DESIGN.md` and\n13\t  `docs/GAME.md` when the work touches the design.\n14\t\n15\tPrompts to give the receiving agent:\n16\t\n17\t> Make a game: read START_HERE.md and docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md, install the Linux packages and\n18\t> build the `pav` CLI. Then make *(your idea, e.g. \"a top-down maze where you collect five\n19\t> keys and escape while ghosts patrol\")* on the engine, starting from the starter game. Give\n20\t> it a bot that wins and the tests from the guide, check it with the capture tools, tell me\n21\t> how to play it, and end with the engine feedback the guide asks for.\n22\t\n23\t> Work on the engine: read START_HERE.md, then follow AGENTS.md. *(the task)*\n24\t\n25\tAgents in auto modes may refuse to unpack an archive or run its scripts unless the prompt\n26\tsays to; say so in the prompt when you hand over an archive.\n27\t\n28\t## Linux setup\n29\tUse a Linux machine with Rust installed through rustup. `rust-toolchain.toml` selects\n30\tRust 1.98.1 automatically. The first build needs network access to download the\n31\ttoolchain and dependencies; `Cargo.lock` pins the dependency versions.\n32\t\n33\tOn Debian / Ubuntu, install the native build and runtime dependencies below. If\n34\talready running as root, omit `sudo`. On a managed machine, ask its administrator\n35\tto install missing packages.\n36\t\n37\t```sh\n38\tsudo apt-get update\n39\tsudo apt-get install -y build-essential pkg-config libasound2-dev libudev-dev \\\n40\t  mesa-vulkan-drivers vulkan-tools xvfb libxkbcommon-x11-0 libx11-xcb1 \\\n41\t  libxcursor1 libxrandr2 libxi6\n42\t```\n43\t\n44\tSimulation tools need no display or GPU. Screenshot tools use Vulkan and can use\n45\tMesa lavapipe (software rendering) on a machine without a GPU. Keep builds on disk\n46\tand limit parallel jobs on machines with little RAM.\n47\t\n48\t## Build and inspect headlessly\n49\t\n50\t```sh\n51\tCARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4 cargo build --locked -p pav_tools --bin pav\n52\t./target/debug/pav help\n53\t./target/debug/pav play scene=starter bot=true seeds=5\n54\t./target/debug/pav capture scene=world ticks=60 width=640 height=360 out=out/pavilion.png\n55\t```\n56\t\n57\t`starter` is the starter game; `world` is the pavilion of demo rooms; `town` is Shardfall's\n58\ttown; `arena` is the wave arena; `lab` is the Menagerie; `level/1` starts the descent. A room\n59\tkey such as `drift` loads that demo by itself. `pav repl` keeps a session across commands:\n60\t\n61\t```sh\n62\tprintf 'load scene=world\\ngoto room=drift\\ncapture width=640 height=360 out=out/drift.png\\n' | ./target/debug/pav repl\n63\t./target/debug/pav levelmap depth=7 nav=true out=out/level7.png\n64\t./target/debug/pav campaign scene=town from=1 to=3 wall=60\n65\tcargo test --locked -p pav_core\n66\t```\n67\t\n68\tInspect the generated images with the agent's image viewer. `docs/REFERENCE.md` describes\n69\tevery tool. `.mcp.json` exposes the same CLI as the `shardfall` MCP server; run the `cargo\n70\tbuild` above before starting an MCP client, because a first build takes longer than clients\n71\twait for a server.\n72\t\n73\t## Run the game window\n74\t\n75\t```sh\n76\tCARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4 cargo build --locked -p pav_app\n77\t./target/debug/shardfall --scene starter # the starter game\n78\t./target/debug/shardfall --scene world   # tech demo\n79\t./target/debug/shardfall --scene town    # Shardfall\n80\t```\n81\t\n82\tWithout a graphical desktop, use `xvfb-run -a` around the game command, or use the\n83\theadless capture tools above. The demo's F2 menu jumps between rooms. Startup\n84\tsettings and hero saves are written next to the game executable.\n85\t\n86\tFor Windows cross-compilation and Wine checks, `scripts/setup-linux.sh` installs the\n87\tadditional tools and `scripts/build-windows.sh` builds the executables.\n88\t\n89\t## The studios (live editing in the window)\n90\t- **Objects:** `scripts/asset-studio.sh`, with `.mcp.studio.json` for the LLM: `assets`,\n91\t  `asset_edit`, `asset_preview`, `asset_spawn` (`docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md`,\n92\t  `docs/ASSET_STUDIO_LIVE.md`).\n93\t- **Animation:** `scripts/animation-studio.sh`, with `.mcp.animation-studio.json` (or `pav\n94\t  live` in a second terminal): `anim_edit`, `anim_preview` (`docs/ANIMATION_STUDIO.md`).\n95\t- **Creatures:** Node 22.18 or newer, then `scripts/creature-studio.sh`; `creature_edit`\n96\t  returns a job to follow with `creature_status` (`docs/CREATURE_STUDIO.md`).\n97\t\n98\tEdits save to readable files and show in the window without a rebuild. `.mcp.json` runs a\n99\tseparate headless session; the studio configurations control the visible window.\n100\t\n101\t## Make another handoff archive\n102\tFrom a Git checkout, `scripts/package-agent.sh` packages the tracked files (with local edits)\n103\tinto `out/agent/shardfall-linux-agent.tar.gz` and a SHA-256 checksum; add new files to Git\n104\tfirst. It leaves out `pavilion-lite/` (an older engine, frozen). `scripts/check-handoff.sh`\n105\tunpacks a fresh archive elsewhere, builds it and plays the starter, as a receiving agent\n106\twould: run it after changing what the archive carries or this page.\n107\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0691f0d6-f08e-44c9-b7bd-c57d7fb96b71","uuid":"0997f7fe-4337-46ae-bfca-769d22916e4d","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:39:39.338Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223859__x35112d/work/WhiffFit-WiiFit-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-223859/engine/START_HERE.md","content":"# Shardfall and the Pavilion engine: start here\n\nA Rust game engine built for AI agents (Pavilion), the hack-and-slash built on it (Shardfall)\nand a pavilion of demo rooms. This page is for an agent receiving the source, from a clone of\nhttps://github.com/michaelcrosato/shardfall or from a handoff archive (no Git history, build\ncaches, saved heroes or executables; `git init` if you want to track your changes).\n\n## Which route\n- **Make a new game on the engine:** read `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md` whole. It is the only page\n  you need to start; it begins from the starter game (`crates/pav_core/src/games/`).\n- **Work on the engine or on Shardfall:** read `docs/DOCTRINE.md`, `AGENTS.md` and\n  `docs/PROGRESS.md` (Claude Code loads them through `CLAUDE.md`); `docs/DESIGN.md` and\n  `docs/GAME.md` when the work touches the design.\n\nPrompts to give the receiving agent:\n\n> Make a game: read START_HERE.md and docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md, install the Linux packages and\n> build the `pav` CLI. Then make *(your idea, e.g. \"a top-down maze where you collect five\n> keys and escape while ghosts patrol\")* on the engine, starting from the starter game. Give\n> it a bot that wins and the tests from the guide, check it with the capture tools, tell me\n> how to play it, and end with the engine feedback the guide asks for.\n\n> Work on the engine: read START_HERE.md, then follow AGENTS.md. *(the task)*\n\nAgents in auto modes may refuse to unpack an archive or run its scripts unless the prompt\nsays to; say so in the prompt when you hand over an archive.\n\n## Linux setup\nUse a Linux machine with Rust installed through rustup. `rust-toolchain.toml` selects\nRust 1.98.1 automatically. The first build needs network access to download the\ntoolchain and dependencies; `Cargo.lock` pins the dependency versions.\n\nOn Debian / Ubuntu, install the native build and runtime dependencies below. If\nalready running as root, omit `sudo`. On a managed machine, ask its administrator\nto install missing packages.\n\n```sh\nsudo apt-get update\nsudo apt-get install -y build-essential pkg-config libasound2-dev libudev-dev \\\n  mesa-vulkan-drivers vulkan-tools xvfb libxkbcommon-x11-0 libx11-xcb1 \\\n  libxcursor1 libxrandr2 libxi6\n```\n\nSimulation tools need no display or GPU. Screenshot tools use Vulkan and can use\nMesa lavapipe (software rendering) on a machine without a GPU. Keep builds on disk\nand limit parallel jobs on machines with little RAM.\n\n## Build and inspect headlessly\n\n```sh\nCARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4 cargo build --locked -p pav_tools --bin pav\n./target/debug/pav help\n./target/debug/pav play scene=starter bot=true seeds=5\n./target/debug/pav capture scene=world ticks=60 width=640 height=360 out=out/pavilion.png\n```\n\n`starter` is the starter game; `world` is the pavilion of demo rooms; `town` is Shardfall's\ntown; `arena` is the wave arena; `lab` is the Menagerie; `level/1` starts the descent. A room\nkey such as `drift` loads that demo by itself. `pav repl` keeps a session across commands:\n\n```sh\nprintf 'load scene=world\\ngoto room=drift\\ncapture width=640 height=360 out=out/drift.png\\n' | ./target/debug/pav repl\n./target/debug/pav levelmap depth=7 nav=true out=out/level7.png\n./target/debug/pav campaign scene=town from=1 to=3 wall=60\ncargo test --locked -p pav_core\n```\n\nInspect the generated images with the agent's image viewer. `docs/REFERENCE.md` describes\nevery tool. `.mcp.json` exposes the same CLI as the `shardfall` MCP server; run the `cargo\nbuild` above before starting an MCP client, because a first build takes longer than clients\nwait for a server.\n\n## Run the game window\n\n```sh\nCARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4 cargo build --locked -p pav_app\n./target/debug/shardfall --scene starter # the starter game\n./target/debug/shardfall --scene world   # tech demo\n./target/debug/shardfall --scene town    # Shardfall\n```\n\nWithout a graphical desktop, use `xvfb-run -a` around the game command, or use the\nheadless capture tools above. The demo's F2 menu jumps between rooms. Startup\nsettings and hero saves are written next to the game executable.\n\nFor Windows cross-compilation and Wine checks, `scripts/setup-linux.sh` installs the\nadditional tools and `scripts/build-windows.sh` builds the executables.\n\n## The studios (live editing in the window)\n- **Objects:** `scripts/asset-studio.sh`, with `.mcp.studio.json` for the LLM: `assets`,\n  `asset_edit`, `asset_preview`, `asset_spawn` (`docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md`,\n  `docs/ASSET_STUDIO_LIVE.md`).\n- **Animation:** `scripts/animation-studio.sh`, with `.mcp.animation-studio.json` (or `pav\n  live` in a second terminal): `anim_edit`, `anim_preview` (`docs/ANIMATION_STUDIO.md`).\n- **Creatures:** Node 22.18 or newer, then `scripts/creature-studio.sh`; `creature_edit`\n  returns a job to follow with `creature_status` (`docs/CREATURE_STUDIO.md`).\n\nEdits save to readable files and show in the window without a rebuild. `.mcp.json` runs a\nseparate headless session; the studio configurations control the visible window.\n\n## Make another handoff archive\nFrom a Git checkout, `scripts/package-agent.sh` packages the tracked files (with local edits)\ninto `out/agent/shardfall-linux-agent.tar.gz` and a SHA-256 checksum; add new files to Git\nfirst. It leaves out `pavilion-lite/` (an older engine, frozen). `scripts/check-handoff.sh`\nunpacks a fresh archive elsewhere, builds it and plays the starter, as a receiving agent\nwould: run it after changing what the archive carries or this page.\n","numLines":107,"startLine":1,"totalLines":107}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01W91S4BkBp3Asoqqb8sKwAY","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
