{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjbxTM1uB7MaKgoMVNeu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Whiff Fit** is finished and everything TASK.md asks for is in the folder, but my earlier \"done\" was premature. When I audited it line by line against PLAN.md and TASK.md, I found small gaps the tests didn't cover. Those are now fixed and tested, and the audit comes back clean.\n\n**Gaps the audit found:**\n- Three library clips the plan names were never used, and three hand-keyed clips never played.\n- Five library props were never placed.\n- Four script lines broke the two-line rule.\n- The intro couldn't be skipped.\n- The island had no channel in the distance, and the Tide set had no cast.\n- There was no `Headless::frame` test, no check of the touch card's words, and no check that the page served to the harness was this game.\n\n**Test results on the final page: 119 passed, 0 failed, across two runs.**\n- The full run gave 114 passed and 5 failed. All 5 came from one test step whose pad press could end before any frame saw it, with machine load at 56.\n- I changed only that test's timing, not the game, and reran the browser suite on the same page: 64 of 64. Core and hub had already passed in the full run.\n- Details are in `notes/last_test_run.txt`.\n\n**Still unverified or open:**\n- **Feel and sound:** no person has played it or heard it. Difficulty was tuned with bots, including a simulated human-like player, and sound was checked by measuring levels.\n- **Devices:** pad, touch and phone were emulated, and \"TV\" means judging 1080p screenshots. The 1080p look was at the title and About screens.\n- **Native window:** it ran for 40 seconds under xvfb with no panic, but that only shows it boots and stays up. Nothing was played in it, and it has no sound here because this machine has no audio device.\n- **`engine/.git`:** I left it in place because the plan only asks to delete caches. If you archive the run with `git add`, the engine may be recorded as an embedded repo instead of its files, so remove `engine/.git` first if that matters. Build caches are gone, and `notes/engine.diff` holds the changes to the engine's existing files.\n- **Hub ready timing:** a copy now also reports ready 12 seconds after connecting, once its canvas exists. The plan's script waited only for the first status, and on a software renderer under load a copy could miss the harness's 20-second limit and be sat out.\n\nOther choices where the plan left room are in `notes/decisions.md`.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 22:38:59\n- **Source:** Wii Fit\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** sports mini-game anthology with a light life-sim frame (daily ritual, unlocks, wardrobe). Third person, with a camera per activity: 3/4 follow on the island, mirror-style front view for exercises, chase for the lap, paddle and tray, high 3/4 for the raft, side view for the dives.\n- **Tech:**\n  - Rust 1.98.1 compiled to WebAssembly with WebGPU.\n  - Pavilion engine (Shardfall repository, commit 49ef2ba, changed for this game): rapier3d 0.36 physics, wgpu renderer, egui 0.36 painter UI, cpal audio, gilrs gamepads.\n  - wasm-bindgen 0.2.129, AI Hub session client 1.4.0.\n  - One self-contained HTML file with no external dependencies.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's cel-shaded rounded primitives with thin ink outlines, taken high-key and pastel: 37 hand-written props, nine sets, a painted UI in Fredoka and Nunito.\n- **Animation:** the engine's procedural puppet with motion clips: 25 hand-keyed clips plus library clips from Mesh2Motion, CMU, 100STYLE and Quaternius. Poses are scrubbed by gameplay, and the whole figure leans about the feet.\n- **Audio:** all in code with no audio files: six sequenced songs (bossa, pads, waltz, funk-pop, mallets, march), 43 synthesized sound effects and stingers, Animalese-style gibberish voices from formant-filtered blips with a timbre per speaker. Music is at 10% by default.\n- **Online:** AI Hub session (ghost mode, 1 to 4 players): the Inter-Resort Medley with other players as seat-coloured ghosts in neighbouring lanes. On its own, Play Together passes one pad among 2 to 4 guests, and Yesterday You replays your own best runs as ghosts.\n- **Testing:**\n  - Headless only: Rust tests with a bot that plays New Stay to the credits, each event on five seeds, and balance tables with a human-like player.\n  - Playwright in Chromium with a stub pad, keys, mouse, and phone and tablet profiles, plus WebKit with an iPhone profile.\n  - The AI Hub harness at 2 to 4 players on a bad network and with the host leaving.\n  - Captures for composition, and a 40-second smoke run of the native window under xvfb.\n  - No physical controller, touch device, TV or second person, and nobody listened to the sound.\n- **Research:** none beyond the plan's own short web search on Wii Fit. I read the engine's docs and source and the AI Hub protocol.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** added on import\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** 25 issues, counted in `notes/feedback.md` as I went.\n- **How many were critical?** 4:\n  - the page ran under 2 ticks a second because of per-tile water zones;\n  - figures fell through decks and posts with no colliders;\n  - the player was left behind after the Morning Check, so the island showed empty sky;\n  - the attract bot kept closing Settings on touch-only devices.\n- **Most helpful test?** The bot playing New Stay to the credits on three seeds, with the balance table beside it: bot, human-ish, shaky and idle scores per event. It showed six events giving 1000 to anyone.\n- **Least helpful test?** Native capture timings on a machine shared with other agents (load 39 on 32 cores): they said nothing about rendering cost.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **Liked most?** Everything runs headless and the same sim plays in tests, tools and the browser. A bot playing the whole game in seconds changed how I worked. Room files plus hand-written JSON props made nine sets cheap.\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong?** Yes.\n  - A water zone per tile looked harmless but cost almost the whole frame, through `merge_zones` and a wave grid per rectangle.\n  - Room cameras override cues set in the same frame, so I dropped them.\n  - `pav load` dropped a scene's first cue.\n  - Props with no solid parts silently have no collider, which sank my figures.\n  - I also guessed the `AiDef` form and several prop schema rules.\n- **Most important thing built?** The UI as data built in the sim (a node list at virtual 1080p drawn by egui), with menus as data, so pad, keys, mouse, tests and the bot share one path. Next, the Balance Core and the sequencer with its song clock.\n- **Hardest part?** Multiple sets in one sim. The engine assumes one room or a streaming world, so the sets, the camera per state, culling the other sets and event-placed figures all needed care. `pav_app`'s frame function is long, and game modes thread through it as flags.\n- **What changed in the engine, and why?** The game needed input, UI, sound and multi-set support the engine didn't have, so these all changed:\n  - `input.rs` (lean, any, ui, song_pos, new buttons)\n  - `frame.rs` (status, fit UI, camera yaw, new events)\n  - `games/mod.rs` (frozen, page status, UI frame, camera yaw, extra objects)\n  - `sim.rs` (frozen ticks, show only the current room)\n  - `puppet.rs` (whole-figure tilt)\n  - `parts.rs` (Handle)\n  - `pav_audio` (buses, formants, LFO, sequencer, song clock)\n  - `pav_view` (BigSplash, Wake, Confetti)\n  - `pav_app` (input layout, lean shaping, weblink, hub ghosts)\n  - `pav_tools` (t_input lean, cue fix on load)\n  - `world.rs` (only_current)\n  - `course.rs` (no engine splash when a game places its figures)\n- **One change?** A first-class set or stage concept: several rooms on one sim, only the current one simulated at full cost and drawn, each with its own camera and view, switched with one call. Most of my integration work was reinventing that.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **Liked most?** The plan was specific where it mattered, with verified recipes for the hard seams: the hub block, the controller stub, the sound patch and the build times. It let me spend my effort on the game rather than on finding out how.\n- **Confusing?** Little. The brief says the plan's section numbers map to its items, and the plan says the brief wins on names, so the two read together well. Whether arrow keys should both walk and step needed a decision.\n- **Hardest?** Judging feel with no hands: tuning how a lean, a squat or a splash feels from bots, numbers and contact sheets. I wrote a human-like player to stand in, but nobody has held the pad or heard the sound.\n- **Would change?** Allow a final pass by a person, or an instruction to report feel as untested. I would also add that a shared machine may run far slower than the plan's measured ticks per second, so browser tests should have a fast mode from the start.\n\n## Files\nEverything is in `work/`:\n- `WhiffFit-WiiFit-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-223859.html` (the game, 12.1 MB, opens directly from disk)\n- `exit-interview.json` (parses, and matches the in-game About screen)\n- `PROGRESS.md`\n- `WhiffFit-WiiFit-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-223859/` (dev folder: source, tests, tools and notes; start with its `README.md`)"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4371,"cache_read_input_tokens":918415,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4371},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0691f0d6-f08e-44c9-b7bd-c57d7fb96b71","uuid":"f37a247e-7288-436e-97d3-b17614ad1690","timestamp":"2026-11-05T10:13:44.467Z","request_id":"req_011CgjbxSvxVqy9JptCi5iQ7"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says. Done when: Everything in PLAN.md that can be done on this computer is done, and everything TASK.md asks you to hand in is in this folder.]: The transcript shows STAGE.md read and the TASK.md deliverables present on disk, but PLAN.md's requirements are not fully satisfied. The final message states that the work is done, yet it lists open items: no person has played or heard the game, the native window was never played (only a 40-second boot under xvfb), and engine/.git was left in place. The 119-passed count combines a full run with 5 failures and a later browser-only rerun after changing the test timing, so the final page was not shown to pass the complete suite in one run. The audit also named but did not confirm the plan's demand that the game be a single html file bundled without the dev folder, and the about-data check passed but the title was not reconfirmed after the last build."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0691f0d6-f08e-44c9-b7bd-c57d7fb96b71","uuid":"6fb0f002-d21e-417d-b6fc-ccec2ce189e7","timestamp":"2026-11-05T10:13:46.810Z","isSynthetic":true}
