plash, Wake, Confetti); pav_app (fit input layout, lean shaping, weblink, hub ghosts); pav_tools (t_input lean, cue fix on load); world.rs (only_current); course.rs (no engine splash when a game places its figures).\n- A first-class set or stage concept: several rooms on one sim, only the current one simulated at full cost and drawn, each with its own camera and view, switched with one call. Most of my integration work was reinventing that.\n== promptFeedback\n- The plan was specific where it mattered, with verified recipes for the hard seams: the hub block, the controller stub, the sound patch and the build times. It let me spend my effort on the game rather than on finding out how.\n- Little. The brief says the plan's section numbers map to its items, and the plan says the brief wins on names, so the two read together well. Whether arrow keys should both walk and step needed a decision.\n- Judging feel with no hands: tuning how a lean, a squat or a splash feels from bots, numbers and contact sheets. I wrote a human-like player to stand in, but nobody has held the pad or heard the sound.\n- Allow a final pass by a person or an instruction to report feel as untested. I would also add that a shared machine may run far slower than the plan's measured ticks per second, so browser tests should have a fast mode from the start.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0181PXVPinh7w4ztT8MrxeCT","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
